                                   SBOM projects.
                                   Builds pass --lockfile automatically when the file exists;
                                   MALF_LOCKFILE_STRICT=1 drops --lockfile-partial so an
                                   unlocked dependency is a hard error (CI / release legs).
    slot    [status|acquire|release]
                                   the WORKSPACE build slot — one LANE builds at a time, across
                                   every repo and across the gaps between its runs (this is not
                                   the per-build-directory lock, which malf takes for itself).
                                   The stamp carries an ANCHOR (the holder's session pid + that
                                   pid's start time, so "is the holder alive?" has an answer even
                                   when no malf process is running) and a TOKEN (32 hex, so
                                   "am I the holder?" has one too).
                                   THE ANCHOR'S RESOLUTION IS A SESSION, NOT A LANE: between two
                                   lanes of ONE session it proves the session alive and nothing
                                   about the holding lane, so a lane that died mid-run reads
                                   HELD-and-alive. There is no per-lane pid to anchor on, so this
                                   is DECLARED rather than fixed — every command detects the case
                                   and says so, and withdraws the kill-the-anchor advice with it.
                                   The token half is unaffected.
                                   acquire  claims it, or REFUSES and names the live holder;
                                            a holder whose anchor is provably gone is reclaimed
                                            automatically, and said so out loud
                                   release  --token <token from acquire/status>; --force only for
                                            a slot with no readable stamp. A LIVE holder cannot be
                                            forced — kill its anchor pid, then the slot frees
                                            itself, EXCEPT when that anchor is your own session's
                                            (see above), where killing it takes every sibling lane
                                            and the token is the only escape.
                                            Never 'rm -rf' the directory.
                                   status   FREE / HELD / STALE / UNKNOWN, and exits 0 / 1 / 2 / 3
                                   Path: MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR (default /tmp/coderoast-build-slot).
    sbom    [--check]              regenerate each published CycloneDX SBOM from the RESOLVED
                                   conan graph (malf/sbom_gen.py). --check writes nothing and
                                   fails if a committed SBOM has drifted from the graph.
    run     <exe-name> [args...]
    bin-dir [subdir] [--asan|--profile <name>]   print the PROFILE-keyed build dir for a target

Verbs (one compile surface):
    `build` compiles EVERYTHING — the package + its tests + its benches.
    `test` and `bench` run (ctest / the bench executables) on top of the same
    incremental build, so what runs is always what you just built.

Targets:
    [target] omitted     every package under the current dir, DEPENDENCY-ORDERED
                         (mono-package root = the single package; a multi-package
                         repo root or the workspace root = the whole set)
    [target] = pkg dir   exactly that package (also from inside the dir itself —
                         a package subfolder behaves like a mono-package repo)
    Build trees are package-anchored: <pkg>/build-<profile-key> — ONE tree per
    (package, profile) whether built as target, editable dep, or from a subfolder.

Build profiles (--profile <name>, registry in malf/profiles/):
    --profile selects any registry profile; build type + a clean build-context for
    sanitizer profiles are derived from the profile. `--asan` is sugar for the ASan
    profile. `malf profiles` lists them.

Cache:
    Local  : MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT/.conan2  (or CONAN_HOME)

Local dev dependency bootstrap:
    build/test/bench register sibling workspace packages as Conan editables, then
    run conan install + CMake/Ninja for those editable deps in dependency
    order. This keeps rebuilds incremental and lets CMakeDeps find libraries
    from each dependency's package-anchored build tree.
    The set is the BUILD closure, which is wider than what the target links: the
    target's transitive workspace requires, PLUS the workspace test_requires of
    every member in it. Each dep is configured as the top-level project of its own
    cmake preset, so its tests and benches are ON and its own test_requires must
    resolve too.
    Override the scan root with MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT or disable with MALF_AUTO_WORKSPACE_DEPS=0.

Editables:
    malf registers workspace editables automatically, but only for a build
    target's dependencies. 'malf bump' re-syncs every cache for you; run
    'malf editables sync' standalone only to clear the registry and re-register
    every workspace package at its current conanfile version out of band (e.g.
    after a manual recipe edit). 'malf editables list' shows the current registry.
    Manual equivalent of one registration:
    conan editable add <path> --name=<name> --version=<version> -of <path>
    Clean all registrations with 'malf clean editables'.

Profile:
    $CONAN_HOME/profiles/linux-clang21-libcxx-release  (dev default; or MALF_PROFILE_NAME)
  gcc-16 (ship + determinism reference leg): --profile linux-gcc16-release
  clang-21 + libstdc++ (keyed):              --profile linux-clang21-release
EOF
}

cmd_profiles() {
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _malf_refuse_option profiles "$1"
    echo "malf build profiles (registry: malf/profiles/) — use: malf build <subdir> --profile <name>"
    local seen=" " d f n desc
    for d in "$MALF_DIR/profiles" "$MALF_DIR"; do
        [[ -d "$d" ]] || continue
        for f in "$d"/linux-*; do
            [[ -f "$f" ]] || continue
            n="$(basename "$f")"
            case "$seen" in *" $n "*) continue ;; esac
            seen="$seen$n "
            desc="$(grep -m1 -E '^#' "$f" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^#[[:space:]]*//')"
            printf "  %-24s %s\n" "$n" "$desc"
        done
    done
    echo "  (--asan == --profile $MALF_ASAN_PROFILE)"
}

# Print the build directory for a target under the active profile. Lets scripts locate built
# binaries without hardcoding build/<pkg> (which breaks under --asan etc.):
#   "$(malf bin-dir --asan fuzz)/parse_fuzzer"
# THE HEADER USED TO SAY "build-type-keyed" AND THAT WAS NEVER TRUE: _malf_build_dir keys on the
# PROFILE alone. The claim was harmless only while nothing depended on it, and it is the same
# misreading `N120` is about — a name that encodes one axis of a configuration gets read as
# encoding all of them.
cmd_bin_dir() {
    local subdir=""
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --profile) shift; _malf_apply_profile "$1" ;;
            --asan)    _malf_apply_profile "$MALF_ASAN_PROFILE" ;;
            -*)        _malf_refuse_option bin-dir "$1" ;;
            *)         subdir="$1" ;;
        esac
        shift
    done
    local target
    target="$(_malf_resolve_target "$subdir")" || exit 1
    _malf_build_dir "$target"
}

# Coordinated workspace version bump: rewrite every version axis to X.Y.Z via
# pin_coherence's `bump` engine, then re-run the coherence check to prove it
# (bump rewrites exactly what the check validates → GREEN by construction). Then
# bring the conan world along with the recipes — re-sync editables to the new
# baseline across EVERY keyed cache, then surgically prune the now-stale (old-
# version) first-party binaries. Both were the recurring post-bump footgun left
# to human memory — editable version skew after a bump, and cache hygiene across
# multiple conan homes; chaining them in makes a bump leave a
# coherent workspace by construction, never one cache behind. sift-action is
# deliberately OUT of scope — its engine pin tracks published releases, not the
# line being opened, and `sift-action/bump.sh` owns it (see the note at the end).
# Tested for 1.5.0; from then on the founder owns pulling this trigger.
cmd_bump() {
    local target="${1:-}"
    case "$target" in -*) _malf_refuse_option bump "$target" ;; esac
    if [[ ! "$target" =~ ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        echo "malf bump: usage: malf bump X.Y.Z   (coordinated workspace version bump)" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    local pin="$MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT/scripts/pin_coherence.py"
    if [[ ! -f "$pin" ]]; then
        echo "malf bump: $pin not found — run from a workspace checkout" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    echo "── malf bump $target — rewriting every workspace version axis ──"
    python3 "$pin" bump "$target" "$MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT" || return 1

    # ── Staleness hygiene, chained in so it is never left to memory. The bump above
    # rewrote every recipe to $target, but the conan world is still one version behind:
    # each keyed cache's editable registry holds orphaned OLD-version refs (conan
    # editable add refuses a recipe-mismatched version, so the prior ref lingers), and
    # the previous version's first-party BINARIES sit in every cache as inert disk bloat
    # + a footgun for any raw (non-malf) conan call. Re-sync editables to the new baseline
    # across EVERY keyed cache FIRST — the lock below resolves through the registry, and
    # clean stale must run against a registry already coherent at the baseline it derives
    # from the recipes.
    echo "── re-syncing editables to $target across every keyed cache ──"
    _malf_editables_sync || return 1

    # ── The plain lock, chained where its own doc already prescribed it ──
    # A bump moves every first-party VERSION, and first-party entries in conan.lock carry
    # one — so every bump desynchronises the lockfile from the recipes (the CAREFUL note on
    # cmd_lock: measured at the 1.8.4 and 1.8.5 bumps as red tags, then again as INV-14 once
    # the gate learned to look). "The fix after a bump is a plain `malf lock`" was that
    # note's instruction to the operator; leaving it to the operator is the same
    # step-to-forget class that got the SBOM chained. Plain lock, NEVER --update: plain is
    # behaviour-neutral (first-party versions only, third-party untouched), while --update
    # re-resolves third-party against the REMOTES — upstream movement that wants a
    # reviewable diff and a gate re-run, which belongs at the cut head (OPS-1.S15), not on
    # every bump. cmd_lock chains the SBOM re-derivation itself, and the measured ordering
    # lesson holds by construction here: the registry was re-synced above, so the projected
    # graph reads $target (the EDITABLE REGISTRY decides resolved first-party, not the lock
    # — measured at 1.8.8 with a 1.8.7 lock).
    cmd_lock || return 1

    echo "── pruning stale (!= $target) first-party packages from every cache ──"
    _malf_clean_stale || return 1

    # ── Verification LAST, judging the finished state — never the half-built one ──
    # This check ran directly after the rewrite until 2026-08-15, and INV-14 made that
    # ordering self-defeating: post-rewrite the lockfile STILL names the old version by
    # construction, so the full-coherence check was structurally red at that point in the
    # chain — the 1.9.4 bump exited there and STRANDED the re-sync, the prune and the SBOM,
    # leaving every cache one version behind (the next `malf lock --update` refused 19
    # roots). Sitting last, a green means the whole post-bump state is coherent, and a red
    # indicts the state rather than the chain's own ordering.
    echo "── verifying pin coherence at $target ──"
    python3 "$pin" "$MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT" || return 1

    # ── sift-action is NOT part of a platform bump, by design ────────────────
    # This used to rewrite src/sift-version.ts to $target and re-bundle dist/index.js.
    # It cannot: the engine binary for $target is published by the eidos Release during
    # $target's OWN cut, so between the post-cut bump and the next cut the pin named a
    # release that did not exist. The result was a commit that could not be pushed
    # without 404-ing every consumer at download — sift-action sat deliberately dirty
    # for a whole cycle, and the cut runbook carried it as a standing preflight
    # exception ("expected to be behind").
    #
    # The Action's engine pin is derived from what has been PUBLISHED, not from the line
    # we are opening, so it is not a workspace axis at all. `sift-action/bump.sh` owns
    # it end to end — resolve the latest published engine, refuse one whose binary and
    # .sha256 are not both live, rewrite the source pin and the Jenkins example, and
    # repackage the bundle (this step, moved to where the pin now changes). Run it after
    # an engine cut publishes, then cut an Action release and re-point @v1.
}

# Pre-cut Release Gate: forced `conan create` for EVERY released package (the
# `release: true` set in packages.yml), in dependency order, from a CLEAN EXPORT.
# Catches the conan-create-only drift class — a rotted test_package, or a sibling
# package the editable `malf test <repo>` never compiles — BEFORE the tag, not AT
# it (the v1.6.2 eidos break). The released set is the ONE canonical source
# (scripts/pin_coherence.py released, from packages.yml), shared with the tag-time
# Release Gate — no second hand-maintained list to drift out of step with it.
#
# Non-vacuity is the whole point of the gate:
#   * --build="<pkg>/*" FORCES each package from source even when a cached binary
#     exists; plain --build=missing CACHE-SKIPS it (0 compile steps → vacuous exit 0).
#   * conan create (not the editable build) is the ONLY path that exports to the
#     cache and compiles the test_package consumer smoke + a package's tests from a
#     clean export — exactly what `malf test`/editables never build.
#   * a DEDICATED, editable-FREE cache: first-party deps resolve from the cache
#     packages THIS sweep builds in dep order, never from a working-tree editable
#     (which would shadow the clean export the gate exists to exercise). The dev's
#     editable registry is untouched. Override the cache with MALF_CUT_VERIFY_CONAN_HOME
#     (CI points it at the warm dep cache so only first-party packages rebuild).
#
# Usage: malf cut-verify [pkg-name] [--profile <name>] [--list]
#   pkg-name   scope the sweep to one released package (else: the whole set)
#   --list     print the canonical released set (dep order) and exit — build nothing
# malf inventory [dir] — the ADR-3.D9 gate, standalone. Lints the WHOLE workspace (an orphan
# anywhere is a defect, and the predicate is workspace-wide by nature), then configures and
# builds every inventory entry of the resolved repo to a LINKED artifact in ONE canonical cell.
#
# ⚠ THE BOUNDARY IS DECLARED, NOT DISCOVERED (ADR-3.D9): this proves the harnesses BUILD. The
# determinism matrices (canon scripts/det_public_proof.sh, metalog
# scripts/determinism_bitidentity.sh) prove they are DETERMINISTIC, at the tag. A digest drift
# still first appears at the cut, BY DESIGN — do not widen this into a determinism sweep to make
# the daily gate feel complete.
# The cell COUNT is deliberately not restated here: it said "8-cell" until 2026-09-04, when both
# sweeps retired an inert -ffp-contract axis and halved. A number in a second file is a number that
# drifts, and this sentence needs the boundary, not the arithmetic.
cmd_inventory() {
    local subdir=""
    # The SAME flag grammar as build/test — not a subset. A verb that silently ignored
    # `--profile` would run the wrong leg and report success for it, which is the species of
    # false claim this whole file exists to remove.
    # This used to seed Release, described as "an inventory cell is a Release compile-and-link
    # check". A compile-and-link check is a check of the leg you asked for: under `--asan` the
    # cell's conan install already resolved at the profile's Debug (it passes no -s build_type),
    # while the cmake configure was handed Release — the two halves of one cell disagreeing.
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --profile) shift; _malf_apply_profile "$1" ;;
            --asan)    _malf_apply_profile "$MALF_ASAN_PROFILE" ;;
            -*) _malf_refuse_option inventory "$1" ;;
            *) subdir="$1" ;;
        esac
        shift
    done
    local root; root="$(_malf_resolve_dir "$subdir")" || exit 1
    _malf_use_keyed_cache
    MALF_SWEEP="" MALF_SKIP_INVENTORY="" _malf_run_inventory "$root"
}

cmd_cut_verify() {
    local only="" list_only=false
    # A RELEASE gate builds the way the cut SHIPS: gcc-16 / libstdc++ — the canonical
    # conan-create environment (release-publish + the conan-module action default), not
    # the dev-default clang/libc++. Overridable with --profile (e.g. a clang-only box).
    _malf_apply_profile "${MALF_CUT_VERIFY_PROFILE:-linux-gcc16-release}"
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --profile) shift; _malf_apply_profile "$1" ;;
            --list)    list_only=true ;;
            --only)    shift; only="$1" ;;
            -*)        _malf_refuse_option cut-verify "$1" ;;
            *)         only="$1" ;;   # positional scope filter: a package NAME
        esac
        shift
    done

    local pin="$MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT/scripts/pin_coherence.py"
    [[ -f "$pin" ]] || { echo "malf cut-verify: $pin not found — run from a workspace checkout" >&2; return 1; }

    # The canonical released-package list, topo-ordered (deps before dependents).
    local released
    released="$(python3 "$pin" released "$MALF_WORKSPACE_ROOT")" \
        || { echo "malf cut-verify: failed to resolve the released-package list" >&2; return 1; }

    if $list_only; then
        echo "malf cut-verify: canonical released set (packages.yml release:true, dep order):"
        printf '%s\n' "$released" | awk -F'\t' 'NF>=2 {printf "  %-30s %s\n", $1, $2}'
        return 0
    fi

    # ── Dedicated, editable-free clean-export cache (see header) ──────────────
    export CONAN_HOME="${MALF_CUT_VERIFY_CONAN_HOME:-$MALF_CONAN_HOME_BASE/cut-verify}"
    mkdir -p "$CONAN_HOME/profiles"
    _malf_sync_conan_conf
    _malf_apply_profile "$MALF_PROFILE_NAME"      # re-resolve the profile INTO this cache
    rm -f "$CONAN_HOME/editable_packages.json"    # CLEAN-EXPORT invariant: no editables
    # CLEAN-EXPORT invariant #2: recipes branch their cmake builddirs on MALF_EDITABLE_BUILD_DIR
    # (e.g. canon/eidos ship their OWN cmake config under lib/cmake/<pkg> with cmake_find_mode=none;
    # when the var is set they ALSO list the editable build-tree dir). A clean conan create has no
    # editable build tree, so that path is bogus and pollutes CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH → find_package fails.
    # Real CI (conan-module / release-publish) runs with it UNSET; match that exactly. _malf_apply_profile
    # exported it just above, so unset it here, after.
    unset MALF_EDITABLE_BUILD_DIR

    echo "=== malf cut-verify: forced conan create over the released set ($MALF_PROFILE_NAME) ==="
    echo "    cache: $CONAN_HOME"

    local profile_args=(
        --profile:host="$MALF_PROFILE"
        --profile:build="$MALF_BUILD_PROFILE"
        # The profile's own build_type, not a literal `Release`. The default cut-verify profile
        # linux-gcc16-release declares Release, so this is byte-identical there; it stops being
        # identical the moment someone passes `--profile` (the header offers exactly that for a
        # clang-only box), where a hardcoded Release would configure a build type the named
        # profile does not declare.
        -s build_type="$MALF_CONFIG"
    )

    local name path deps total=0 ok=0
    local -a failed=() skipped=()
    local -A broke=()   # name -> 1 once it failed or was skipped (blocks dependents)

    while IFS=$'\t' read -r name path deps; do
        [[ -n "$name" && -n "$path" ]] || continue
        if [[ -n "$only" && "$name" != "$only" && "$(basename "$path")" != "$only" ]]; then
            continue
        fi
        total=$((total+1))

        # SKIP (don't FAIL) a package whose first-party dep already broke — its
        # conan create would fail for a MISSING upstream, not a defect of its own.
        # Reporting it as "blocked" keeps cascade noise out of the failure list.
        local blocked="" dep
        for dep in ${deps//,/ }; do
            [[ -n "${broke[$dep]:-}" ]] && { blocked="$dep"; break; }
        done
        if [[ -n "$blocked" ]]; then
            skipped+=("$name (dep $blocked failed)")
            broke["$name"]=1
            echo "--- cut-verify: SKIP $name — upstream $blocked failed ---" >&2
            continue
        fi

        echo "::group::cut-verify [$total] conan create $name ($path)"
        # --build=missing: third-party + not-yet-built first-party deps build if absent
        #   (deps earlier in dep order are already in-cache from THIS sweep → current).
        # --build="$name/*": FORCE this package from source (kills the cache-skip).
        if conan create "$path" "${profile_args[@]}" --build=missing --build="$name/*"; then
            ok=$((ok+1)); echo "::endgroup::"
        else
            echo "::endgroup::"
            failed+=("$name"); broke["$name"]=1
            echo "::error::cut-verify FAIL — conan create $name ($path) failed" >&2
        fi
    done <<< "$released"

    echo
    if [[ $total -eq 0 ]]; then
        echo "malf cut-verify: no released package matched '$only'" >&2; return 1
    fi
    if [[ ${#failed[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
        echo "❌ malf cut-verify: ${#failed[@]} released package(s) FAILED forced conan create: ${failed[*]}" >&2
        [[ ${#skipped[@]} -gt 0 ]] && echo "   blocked (upstream failed): ${skipped[*]}" >&2
        echo "   A failure here is a conan-create-only break (rotted test_package / sibling package) that" >&2
        echo "   'malf test' + editables never compile — fix at root BEFORE the cut." >&2
        return 1
    fi
    echo "✅ malf cut-verify: all $ok released package(s) conan-create clean —"
    echo "   forced from-source rebuild + test_package, dependency order, clean export ($MALF_PROFILE_NAME)."
}

# ─── The workspace build slot ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHAT IT IS, and how it differs from the build lock above. Concurrent malf runs share ONE
# editable conan tree and one set of package-anchored build trees, so the workspace rule is that
# at most one LANE builds at a time (CLAUDE.md § Build system). `_malf_with_build_lock` serializes
# two runs that reach the same build DIRECTORY and is held for the length of one build. This is
# the coarser, longer-lived thing: a workspace-wide claim a lane holds across many runs AND across
# the gaps between them, so a second lane knows not to start one.
#
# WHY IT NEEDED A TOKEN — measured 2026-09-03. The protocol was hand-rolled and lived nowhere in
# this file: `mkdir /tmp/coderoast-build-slot`, a `holder` file carrying the current run's pid,
# re-stamped on every run. A third party judged the slot stale and `rm -rf`'d it WHILE a `malf
# test` was live; the holder's next re-stamp failed and two gcc runs then ran unprotected. They
# were green and consistent with their clang twins, so nothing was corrupted — a near miss, not a
# loss. The root cause is not the deletion. It is that a bare mkdir carries NO OWNERSHIP PROOF, so
# a lane cannot tell a corpse from a holder that is merely between runs, and a lane that guesses
# wrong deletes a directory somebody else owns.
#
# TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS. Conflating them is exactly what the one `holder`
# file did, which is why it could answer neither:
#   "Is the holder still alive?"  ->  the ANCHOR: a pid plus that pid's start time.
#   "Am I the holder?"            ->  the TOKEN: 32 random hex, minted at acquisition.
#
# THE ANCHOR IS THE LANE'S SESSION, NEVER THE RUN, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REPAIR. A per-run pid is
# gone between runs BY DESIGN, so "that pid is dead" carries no information at all — which is not
# a hypothetical: while this was being written /tmp/coderoast-build-slot held `holder` 707217
# (gone) and `owner` hephaistos-N147, and whether that lane was finished or thinking was
# unknowable from the directory. The anchor is instead the acquiring process's nearest ancestor
# that is neither a shell nor malf: the session leader behind an interactive shell at a desk, the
# agent process behind a lane, the job's worker under CI. It outlives every individual run and
# dies with the thing that was actually holding the slot, which is the only death that means the
# slot is free.
#
# WHY NOT A HEARTBEAT OR A LEASE. A lease reclaims on silence, and a lane that is legitimately
# idle between runs — reading, writing docs, waiting on a review — is silent. Its length would be
# a guess trading a false reclaim against a long stall, and it would have to be re-guessed every
# time the work changed shape. The anchor answers the real question and has no number to tune.
#
# THE ANCHOR'S RESOLUTION IS A SESSION, NOT A LANE, AND BETWEEN TWO LANES OF ONE SESSION IT
# ANSWERS NOTHING. Measured twice. 2026-09-03: a finishing lane and the slot's holder both derived
# anchor pid 3053. 2026-09-05, re-derived deliberately from a THIRD, unrelated lane: it walked to
# pid 3053 with start 1788357646, byte-identical to the stamp a different lane had written. The
# cause is structural and not a bug in the walk — a delegated lane is not an OS process. Its
# ancestry is `bash -> claude`, the bash is per-run and dies between runs (which is the defect the
# anchor was introduced to fix), and the `claude` above it is the whole SESSION. There is no third
# object, so THERE IS NO PER-LANE PID TO ANCHOR ON: this is not a matter of choosing a better
# ancestor. The consequence: had the holding lane died mid-run, a sibling still alive would keep
# the corpse reading HELD-and-alive for as long as the session lived. It also explains the five
# dead lanes on 2026-09-03 whose slots looked held.
#
# THE TOKEN HALF IS UNAFFECTED — it still answers "am I the holder?" correctly, so no third party
# can release by accident and nothing was ever at risk from this.
#
# SO THIS IS DECLARED, NOT FIXED, AND THE DECLARATION IS CONDITIONAL RATHER THAN A BLANKET
# DISCLAIMER. `_malf_slot_anchor_is_shared` compares the READER's own derived anchor against the
# stamped one; equal means the aliveness proof is void FOR THIS READER, and the message fires
# exactly then. A reader in a different session gets a correct answer to the anchor's real
# question ("is the holder's session alive?") and is told nothing extra. No new identifier is
# needed for this — the comparison uses only what the file already computes.
#
# AND IT REMOVES A REMEDY THAT WAS ACTIVELY DANGEROUS UNDER THAT CONDITION. Three code paths told
# a blocked reader to "kill pid <anchor>". When the anchor is shared, that pid is the reader's OWN
# session: following the instruction literally kills the reader and every sibling lane with it.
# Under a shared anchor the only non-catastrophic escape is the TOKEN, which `malf slot status`
# already prints and the stamp already carries — and taking it is a deliberate human act, because
# establishing that the holding lane is over is exactly what this code can no longer do.
#
# WHY NOT pgrep — measured 2026-09-03. `pgrep -f malf` matches the always-on GitHub Actions runner
# service at /home/windows/actions-runner-malf/ on its DIRECTORY NAME: a permanent false positive
# no pattern refinement reaches, present whether or not anyone is building. At the one moment a
# real run existed the poll returned 7 hits, 1 real, and the real one belonged to a sibling
# session. A process NAME identifies nothing here; a pid plus its start time does.
#
# WHY flock IS RIGHT HERE THOUGH IT WAS WRONG FOR THE LINT SCRATCH (see _malf_lint_open_scratch).
# There the flock would have been held for the RUN's lifetime across a forked fan-out, and a lock
# belongs to the open file description, which children inherit — so an orphaned clang-tidy kept it
# and the probe read ALIVE. Here it is held for the milliseconds of one read-decide-write, no
# child is forked inside it, and its only job is to stop two lanes reclaiming the same dead slot
# at the same instant. Different question, different answer.
#
# AN UNPARSEABLE STAMP MEANS HELD — THE OPPOSITE OF _malf_lint_owner_alive, DELIBERATELY. There, a
# stamp that cannot be read is reported DEAD, because refusing to answer would restore the
# ambiguity that function exists to close and the cost of being wrong is a leaked temp directory.
# Here the cost of being wrong is an unprotected build over a shared editable tree. So a stamp
# this code did not write — a hand-rolled slot, a truncated file — is UNKNOWN, is never reclaimed
# automatically, and needs a human to say `--force`.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated so nobody assumes otherwise: it does not stop `rm -rf`. Nothing
# can. It removes the REASON to reach for one, by making "is the holder alive?" a question with an
# answer, and it makes every removal path print who it is destroying before it does it.

# The pid whose death means the slot is free. Walk up from this process, skipping shells and malf
# itself; the answer is the first ancestor that is neither. If the walk reaches init without
# finding one, the answer is the outermost ancestor below init — the session's own shell — which
# is still strictly longer-lived than any run. Never pid 1: an anchor that cannot die is an anchor
# that never frees the slot. Override with MALF_BUILD_SLOT_ANCHOR for anything this cannot see.
_malf_slot_anchor_pid() {
    if [[ -n "${MALF_BUILD_SLOT_ANCHOR:-}" ]]; then echo "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_ANCHOR"; return 0; fi
    local pid=$$ last=$$ hops=0 st rest ppid comm
    while (( hops < 32 )); do
        hops=$(( hops + 1 ))
        st="$(cat "/proc/$pid/stat" 2>/dev/null)" || break
        # `awk '{print $4}'` is WRONG on this file: field 2 is the comm in parentheses and a comm
        # may contain spaces, which shifts every later field. Everything after the LAST ") " is
        # the fixed part (state, ppid, pgrp, …), and no field there can contain that sequence.
        rest="${st##*') '}"
        ppid="$(awk '{print $2}' <<< "$rest")"
        [[ -n "$ppid" && "$ppid" != 0 && "$ppid" != 1 ]] || break
        comm="$(cat "/proc/$ppid/comm" 2>/dev/null)" || break
        last="$ppid"
        case "$comm" in
            bash|sh|dash|zsh|ksh|fish|malf) pid="$ppid" ;;
            *) echo "$ppid"; return 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    echo "$last"
}

# A process's start time, read exactly as _malf_lint_owner_alive reads it: the mtime of its /proc
# directory. ONE mechanism in this file rather than two that differ by a detail. Its residual is
# stated at that function — one-second resolution, so a pid recycled inside the same second as the
# dead anchor reads as alive. Here that residual fails SAFE: the slot then reads HELD when it is
# free, which stalls a lane and asks a human, where the other direction would hand out a build
# nobody is protecting.
_malf_slot_proc_start() {   # <pid>
    stat -c %Y "/proc/$1" 2>/dev/null
}

# Would THIS process anchor on the pid the stamp names? If so, the stamp's "anchor is ALIVE" is
# true of a process this reader is itself running under, so it proves the SESSION is alive and
# says nothing about the holding LANE. Callers use it to qualify a liveness claim and to withdraw
# the "kill the anchor" remedy, never to change a state: the state is still `held`, correctly —
# an unprovable death is not a death. Requires _malf_slot_read to have run.
_malf_slot_anchor_is_shared() {
    [[ -n "${_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR:-}" ]] || return 1
    [[ "$(_malf_slot_anchor_pid)" == "$_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR" ]]
}

# The three call sites below print this identically, so it is written once. stderr or stdout is
# the caller's choice, because `status` is a report and the other two are refusals.
#
# NO LINE HERE MAY BEGIN "malf slot: token " — that prefix is how `status` emits the MACHINE
# field, and a second line carrying it makes `sed -n 's/^malf slot: token //p'` return two values.
# Caught by writing exactly that extractor while testing this note, which then compared a
# two-line token against the stamp and reported a mismatch on the correct token. The same applies
# to "malf slot: dir " and to any other single-value line a caller might scrape.
_malf_slot_shared_anchor_note() {
    echo "malf slot: ⚠ THE ANCHOR IS SHARED WITH THIS PROCESS — pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR is what THIS"
    echo "malf slot: command would anchor on too. The anchor's resolution is a SESSION, and a"
    echo "malf slot: delegated lane is not an OS process, so between two lanes of one session"
    echo "malf slot: 'the anchor is alive' proves the SESSION is alive and NOT that the holding"
    echo "malf slot: lane is. A lane that died mid-run would read HELD-and-alive indefinitely."
    echo "malf slot: DO NOT kill pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR — it is this session, and killing it takes"
    echo "malf slot: every sibling lane with it. Under a shared anchor the only escape is the"
    echo "malf slot: stamp's token, and taking it is a deliberate act: establishing that the"
    echo "malf slot: holding lane is over is what this command can no longer do for you."
}

# Read the slot. Answers through globals rather than stdout because every caller needs several
# fields at once and a command substitution per field would fork per field.
#   free     the directory does not exist
#   held     stamp parses, anchor alive        -> somebody is building; wait
#   dead     stamp parses, anchor gone         -> reclaimable, and acquire reclaims it
#   unknown  no stamp, or one this did not write -> a human decides; never reclaimed for them
_malf_slot_read() {
    _MALF_SLOT_STATE=free
    _MALF_SLOT_TOKEN=""; _MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR=""; _MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR_START=""
    _MALF_SLOT_LABEL=""; _MALF_SLOT_SINCE=""
    [[ -d "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" ]] || return 0
    _MALF_SLOT_STATE=unknown
    local stamp="$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR/stamp"
    [[ -r "$stamp" ]] || return 0
    local magic k_tok token k_anc anchor k_start start
    read -r magic k_tok token k_anc anchor k_start start _ < "$stamp" || return 0
    [[ "$magic" == "malf-slot-1" && "$k_tok" == "token" && "$k_anc" == "anchor" \
       && "$k_start" == "start" && -n "$token" && -n "$anchor" && -n "$start" ]] || return 0
    _MALF_SLOT_TOKEN="$token"; _MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR="$anchor"; _MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR_START="$start"
    _MALF_SLOT_LABEL="$(sed -n '2s/^label //p' "$stamp")"
    _MALF_SLOT_SINCE="$(sed -n '3s/^since //p' "$stamp")"
    local live=""
    live="$(_malf_slot_proc_start "$anchor")" || true
    if [[ -n "$live" && "$live" == "$start" ]]; then
        _MALF_SLOT_STATE=held
    else
        _MALF_SLOT_STATE=dead
    fi
    return 0
}

# 16 bytes of /dev/urandom, not $RANDOM: bash seeds that from the pid and the clock, and two lanes
# acquiring in the same second are exactly the case that must not collide. `od -N16` reads a fixed
# count, so nothing closes a pipe early and no SIGPIPE reaches a producer under `pipefail`.
_malf_slot_mint_token() {
    od -An -tx1 -N16 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \n'
    echo
}

_malf_slot_write_stamp() {   # <token> <anchor pid> <anchor start> <label>
    {
        # Line 1 is the MACHINE line and its shape is a contract — _malf_slot_read parses it.
        # Lines 2 and 3 are `label ` and `since ` for the same reason. Everything below is for a
        # human who found this directory and has to decide what to do about it WITHOUT reading
        # this file, which is the whole failure the incident turned on.
        echo "malf-slot-1 token $1 anchor $2 start $3"
        echo "label $4"
        echo "since $(date -Is)"
        echo "host $(uname -n)  cwd ${CWD:-$PWD}"
        echo
        echo "THIS DIRECTORY IS THE CODEROAST WORKSPACE BUILD SLOT. One lane builds at a time."
        echo
        echo "  IS THE HOLDER STILL ALIVE?   malf slot status"
        echo "     It reads pid $2 and that pid's start time. A holder with no malf process running"
        echo "     is NORMAL — the slot is held across the gaps between runs, on purpose."
        echo "     THE ANCHOR'S RESOLUTION IS A SESSION, NOT A LANE. If you are a lane of the same"
        echo "     session as this holder, pid $2 is YOUR anchor too and its being alive tells you"
        echo "     nothing about whether this holder is. 'malf slot status' detects that and says so."
        echo
        echo "  RELEASE IT (the holder):     malf slot release --token $1"
        echo
        echo "  DO NOT 'rm -rf' THIS DIRECTORY. Deleting it while its holder builds is how two"
        echo "     unprotected gcc runs happened on 2026-09-03. If the holder is genuinely stuck,"
        echo "     kill its anchor process $2; the next 'malf slot acquire' then reclaims the slot"
        echo "     by itself, because it can prove the holder is gone."
    } > "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR/stamp"
}

cmd_slot() {
    local sub="${1:-status}"
    shift || true
    case "$sub" in
        acquire|release|status) ;;
        -*) _malf_refuse_option slot "$sub" ;;
        *)  echo "malf slot: unknown subcommand '$sub'" >&2; _malf_usage_of slot >&2; exit 1 ;;
    esac

    local label="" token="" force=false
    while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
        case "$1" in
            --label) shift; label="${1:-}"
                     [[ -n "$label" ]] || { echo "malf slot: --label needs a value" >&2; exit 1; } ;;
            --token) shift; token="${1:-}"
                     [[ -n "$token" ]] || { echo "malf slot: --token needs a value" >&2; exit 1; } ;;
            --force) force=true ;;
            *) _malf_refuse_option slot "$1" ;;
        esac
        shift
    done

    # An `rm -rf` runs against this path, so it is validated before anything else touches it: an
    # absolute path, at least two components, no trailing slash. A mis-set MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR
    # must fail loudly here rather than delete something a level up.
    if [[ "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" != /*/?* || "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" == */ ]]; then
        echo "malf slot: error: MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR must be an absolute path of at least two" >&2
        echo "malf slot: components with no trailing slash — got '$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR'." >&2
        echo "malf slot: an 'rm -rf' runs against it, so this refuses rather than guess." >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # No graceful degradation, unlike _malf_with_build_lock. That lock's job is to make builds
    # faster and safer and an unserialized build still builds; this one's only job IS the
    # coordination, and an unserialized coordination primitive is not a weaker one, it is none.
    if ! command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "malf slot: error: flock not found (it ships in util-linux)." >&2
        echo "malf slot: the slot is a coordination primitive — an unserialized one would hand two" >&2
        echo "malf slot: lanes the same slot — so this refuses rather than degrade." >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # The mutex is a plain FILE beside the directory, never inside it: the critical section
    # creates and destroys that directory, so a lock living in it would lose its inode mid-hold
    # and stop excluding anything. Truncating rather than recreating keeps the inode stable across
    # runs, which is what flock's identity is.
    local guard="${MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR}.lock" slot_fd
    if ! mkdir -p "$(dirname "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR")" 2>/dev/null || ! : > "$guard" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "malf slot: error: cannot create the mutex file $guard" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    exec {slot_fd}>"$guard"
    if ! flock -w "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_WAIT_S" "$slot_fd"; then
        echo "malf slot: error: another 'malf slot' held the mutex for more than ${MALF_BUILD_SLOT_WAIT_S}s." >&2
        echo "malf slot: that section is a few milliseconds long, so this means a stuck process:" >&2
        echo "malf slot: 'fuser -v $guard' names it." >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    local slot_rc=0
    case "$sub" in
    acquire)
        _malf_slot_read
        case "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" in
        held)
            echo "malf slot: HELD by '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' since ${_MALF_SLOT_SINCE:-<unknown>} — anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR is ALIVE." >&2
            echo "malf slot: that lane still exists whether or not a malf process is running right now —" >&2
            echo "malf slot: the anchor is its SESSION, and a holder between runs is the normal state." >&2
            echo "malf slot: wait for it. If it is genuinely stuck, kill pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR and re-run this" >&2
            echo "malf slot: command, which will then reclaim the slot itself. Do not rm -rf $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR." >&2
            if _malf_slot_anchor_is_shared; then
                echo "malf slot: token $_MALF_SLOT_TOKEN" >&2
                _malf_slot_shared_anchor_note >&2
            fi
            slot_rc=1
            ;;
        unknown)
            echo "malf slot: $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR exists but carries no stamp this malf wrote — a" >&2
            echo "malf slot: hand-rolled slot, or a truncated one. Its holder can be neither confirmed" >&2
            echo "malf slot: nor disproved, so it is NOT reclaimed automatically. It contains:" >&2
            ls -la "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" >&2 || true
            echo "malf slot: once you have established by other means that nobody holds it:" >&2
            echo "malf slot:   malf slot release --force" >&2
            slot_rc=1
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" == dead ]]; then
                echo "malf slot: reclaiming — the previous holder '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' (anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR, held since ${_MALF_SLOT_SINCE:-<unknown>}) is GONE."
                rm -rf "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR"
            fi
            [[ -n "$label" ]] || label="${MALF_BUILD_SLOT_LABEL:-$(id -un)@$(uname -n):$$}"
            local anchor start tok
            anchor="$(_malf_slot_anchor_pid)"
            start="$(_malf_slot_proc_start "$anchor")" || start=""
            if [[ -z "$start" ]]; then
                echo "malf slot: error: cannot read the start time of anchor pid $anchor, so this slot" >&2
                echo "malf slot: would carry no liveness proof at all. Name a live anchor explicitly:" >&2
                echo "malf slot:   MALF_BUILD_SLOT_ANCHOR=<pid> malf slot acquire" >&2
                slot_rc=1
            else
                tok="$(_malf_slot_mint_token)"
                if ! mkdir "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
                    echo "malf slot: error: could not create $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" >&2
                    slot_rc=1
                else
                    _malf_slot_write_stamp "$tok" "$anchor" "$start" "$label"
                    echo "malf slot: ACQUIRED by '$label' · anchor pid $anchor · token $tok"
                    echo "malf slot: release with:  malf slot release --token $tok"
                    echo "malf slot: acquire ONCE and release ONCE — releasing between runs is what lets a"
                    echo "malf slot: second lane in halfway through your work."
                fi
            fi
            ;;
        esac
        ;;

    release)
        _malf_slot_read
        case "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" in
        free)
            echo "malf slot: already FREE — $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR does not exist, nothing to release."
            ;;
        unknown)
            if $force; then
                echo "malf slot: --force — removing $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR, which carries no stamp this malf"
                echo "malf slot: wrote. For the record, it contained:"
                ls -la "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" || true
                rm -rf "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR"
                echo "malf slot: removed."
            else
                echo "malf slot: refusing — $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR carries no stamp this malf wrote, so there" >&2
                echo "malf slot: is no token to match and no anchor to test. It contains:" >&2
                ls -la "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" >&2 || true
                echo "malf slot: if you have established by other means that nobody holds it, say so:" >&2
                echo "malf slot:   malf slot release --force" >&2
                slot_rc=1
            fi
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ -n "$token" && "$token" == "$_MALF_SLOT_TOKEN" ]]; then
                rm -rf "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR"
                echo "malf slot: RELEASED — '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' (anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR)."
            elif [[ "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" == dead ]] && $force; then
                echo "malf slot: --force — the holder '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' (anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR) is GONE; removing."
                rm -rf "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR"
                echo "malf slot: removed. ('malf slot acquire' would have reclaimed it on its own.)"
            elif [[ "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" == dead ]]; then
                echo "malf slot: the holder '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' (anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR) is GONE, but the" >&2
                echo "malf slot: token given does not match the stamp. Either release it as its owner:" >&2
                echo "malf slot:   malf slot release --token $_MALF_SLOT_TOKEN" >&2
                echo "malf slot: or just take the slot — 'malf slot acquire' reclaims a dead holder itself." >&2
                slot_rc=1
            else
                echo "malf slot: REFUSED — the slot is held by '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' since ${_MALF_SLOT_SINCE:-<unknown>}," >&2
                echo "malf slot: and its anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR is ALIVE. ${token:+The token given does not match. }" >&2
                echo "malf slot: this is the release that must not happen by reflex: a holder with no malf" >&2
                echo "malf slot: process running is between runs, not finished." >&2
                echo "malf slot: if that lane is yours, its token is in $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR/stamp and in 'malf slot status'." >&2
                echo "malf slot: if it is stuck, kill pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR — the slot then frees itself. There is" >&2
                echo "malf slot: deliberately no --force for a LIVE holder: the owner has to die first." >&2
                _malf_slot_anchor_is_shared && _malf_slot_shared_anchor_note >&2
                slot_rc=1
            fi
            ;;
        esac
        ;;

    status)
        # Exit status is the state, so a script can branch on it without parsing prose:
        # 0 free · 1 held (alive) · 2 stale (reclaimable) · 3 unknown (needs a human).
        _malf_slot_read
        echo "malf slot: dir $MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR"
        case "$_MALF_SLOT_STATE" in
        free)
            echo "malf slot: FREE — the directory does not exist."
            slot_rc=0 ;;
        held)
            echo "malf slot: HELD by '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' since ${_MALF_SLOT_SINCE:-<unknown>}"
            echo "malf slot: anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR is ALIVE (started $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR_START, same value as the stamp)"
            echo "malf slot: token $_MALF_SLOT_TOKEN"
            echo "malf slot: a holder with no malf process running is between runs — that is the normal state."
            _malf_slot_anchor_is_shared && _malf_slot_shared_anchor_note
            slot_rc=1 ;;
        dead)
            echo "malf slot: STALE — holder '${_MALF_SLOT_LABEL:-<unlabelled>}' since ${_MALF_SLOT_SINCE:-<unknown>}"
            echo "malf slot: anchor pid $_MALF_SLOT_ANCHOR is GONE, so that lane is over."
            echo "malf slot: 'malf slot acquire' reclaims it — no manual deletion needed."
            slot_rc=2 ;;
        *)
            echo "malf slot: UNKNOWN — the directory exists but carries no stamp this malf wrote,"
            echo "malf slot: so its holder can be neither confirmed nor disproved. It contains:"
            ls -la "$MALF_BUILD_SLOT_DIR" || true
            echo "malf slot: 'malf slot release --force' removes it once you have established by other"
            echo "malf slot: means that nobody holds it."
            slot_rc=3 ;;
        esac
        ;;
    esac

    exec {slot_fd}>&-
    return "$slot_rc"
}

# ─── Dispatch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Test seam. `source`-ing malf with MALF_SOURCE_ONLY=1 stops HERE, so the pure helpers
# (_malf_profile_key, _malf_build_key, …) can be exercised without dispatching a command.
# Placed at the dispatch boundary rather than the top of the file so everything a test
# might call is already defined — the guard skips the ACTION, never the definitions.
# Nothing else reads this variable; it is inert in every real invocation.
if [[ -n "${MALF_SOURCE_ONLY:-}" ]]; then
    return 0 2>/dev/null || exit 0
fi

COMMAND="${1:-help}"
shift || true

# ANY verb: `-h`/`--help` anywhere in its arguments (before a `--`, which hands what follows to
# the benchmark binary) prints THAT verb's usage and exits 0. The verb never runs — see
# _malf_refuse_option for the sweep this replaces.
if [[ "$COMMAND" != help ]]; then
    for _arg in "$@"; do
        case "$_arg" in
            -h|--help) _malf_usage_of "$COMMAND"; exit 0 ;;
            --)        break ;;
        esac
    done
    unset _arg
fi

case "$COMMAND" in
    build)            cmd_build "$@" ;;
    test)             cmd_test "$@" ;;
    lint)             cmd_lint "$@" ;;
    format)           cmd_format "$@" ;;
    commands|compile-commands) cmd_compile_commands "$@" ;;
    bench)            cmd_bench "$@" ;;
    clean)            cmd_clean "$@" ;;
    editables)        cmd_editables "$@" ;;
    profiles)         cmd_profiles "$@" ;;
    bump)             cmd_bump "$@" ;;
    inventory)        cmd_inventory "$@" ;;
    cut-verify)       cmd_cut_verify "$@" ;;
    lock)             cmd_lock "$@" ;;
    slot)             cmd_slot "$@" ;;
    sbom)             cmd_sbom "$@" ;;
    run)              cmd_run "$@" ;;
    bin-dir)          cmd_bin_dir "$@" ;;
    help|-h|--help)   cmd_help ;;
    *)
        echo "malf: unknown command '$COMMAND'" >&2
        cmd_help >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
esac
